
                              PPL ver. 3.0
          Copyright (C) 1994 - Clark Development Company, Inc.
                                9-14-94

                       Highlights of New Features
  ============================================================================


         PPL version 3.0 introduces several extensions to the syntax of
  the language itself. The most significant of which is the addition of
  user defined functions and procedures. The syntax and use of user
  defined functions and procedures follows closely to the Pascal
  programming language. User defined functions and procedures can be
  called as standalone statements. Please see the detailed explanation
  in the section entitled USER DEFINED FUNCTIONS AND PROCEDURES.

         Another extension to the PPL language syntax is the addition of
  a BASIC like SELECT CASE construct. This introduces a very useful and
  convenient way to control program flow for several related execution
  paths. See the section called SELECT CASE for details.

         Perhaps the biggest addition to the functionality of PPL is
  support for DBase III+. Several intrinsic functions and statements
  have been added to support development and management of DBase III+
  files and indexes. More information on DBase can be found in the
  section entitled DBASE.

         Support of the new accounting features in PCBoard has also been
  added to PPL v3.0. PPL Programmers can record charges for activities
  as well as report on what will be charged by PCBoard.

         Version 3.0 also allows access to the message base via scanning
  message headers and reading messages into files.

         Several other miscellaneous intrinsic funtions, statements and
  constants have been added to the language as well. See the section
  entitled MISCELLANEOUS for details on these additions.

         PCBoard 15.2 does require more memory then previous versions.
  Because of this many of you, especially those running Desqview, may
  not have enough memory to run larger PPE's. If you receive an error
  message running a PPE saying something about allocating a variable or
  SCRIPT BUFFER, then you need to increase available memory to PCBoard.
  There are many ways to do this. You can contact your local guru to
  find out how to get more memory.

          Another effect due to increased memory requirements is the
  speed of executing PPEs. To keep PPEs running as fast as your used to,
  adjust your overlay buffer size using OVLSIZE.EXE. A size of 20 should
  be sufficient. If you are running DBase applications you may need to
  increase this to 25 or more. This will increase your memory
  requirements, so play around with it till you reach an acceptable
  level of performace and available memory.

  =============================================================================

  Section I.          User Defined Functions and Procedures


         Using user defined functions and procedures reduces the need
  for GOTO and GOSUB statements. PPL programs can now take advantage of
  the benefits of structured programming. Functions differ from
  procedures in that functions return a value and procedures do not. The
  best way to introduce the syntax used to declare, define and call
  functions and procedures is by looking at an example.

  EXAMPLE 1
  --------

 1  DECLARE PROCEDURE proc1(INTEGER i, STRING str, VAR INTEGER j)
 2
 3  *$USEFUNCS
 4
 5  BEGIN
 6
 7  INTEGER int1,int2
 8  STRING  s1
 9
 10       LET int1 = 1
 11       LET int2 = 2
 12       LET s1   = "Hello Beta testers!"
 13
 14       PRINTLN "The value of int2 before call to proc1 is ",int2
 15
 16       proc1(int1,s1,int2)
 17
 18        PRINTLN "The value of int2  after call to proc1 is ",int2
 19
 20 END
 21
 22
 23 PROCEDURE proc1(INTEGER i,STRING str, VAR INTEGER j)
 24
 25        PRINTLN "I'm in proc1"
 26        LET i = 30
 27        LET j = 15
 28
 29 ENDPROC

         Line 1 introduces the procedure proc1 into the program. A
  procedure/function cannot be used unless it has been declared first.
  This declaration can take place anywhere in the program as long as it
  is before the procedure/function is used or defined. It must also be
  declared with global scope i.e. not inside another function/procedure
  definition. Note the VAR keyword before the third parameter j. This
  will be explained when line 23 is described.

         Line 3 uses the USEFUNCS compiler directive. This is a new
  directive and was introduced so that the main body of a PPE can be
  anywhere in the source file delimited by BEGIN and END keywords. This
  directive is used in conjunction with the BEGIN keyword found on line
  5. The USEFUNCS and BEGIN keywords are not required in the case where
  the main body of the program comes before any function/procedure
  definitions. However, when using functions/procedures it is highly
  recommend to use USEFUNCS and BEGIN. If you forget to include the
  *$USEFUNCS directive and put a functio or peocedure definition before
  the main body, your PPE will not run at all. Lines 6-15 look like any
  other PPE so I won't describe them in detail. Note that an explicit
  END keyword is used. This is not required, but is highly recommended.

         Line 16 is where the procedure proc1 is called/invoked. This
  causes program execution to jump to the code in proc1, passing the
  parameters, and begin executing on line 23. Parameters will be
  implicitly converted to the type the procedure expects. The normal
  implicit type conversion rules which have always been in PPL apply.

         Line 23 is where the DEFINITION of the procedure proc1 begins.
  The parameters must be accompanied by the TYPE information for that
  parameter. The VAR keyword is valid for procedure parameters ONLY. It
  is optional and changes the way the parameter is handled. When the VAR
  keyword is used for a procedure parameter, changes made to that
  parameter within the procedure will be reflected on the variable
  outside of the procedure. In this example the actual parameter int2
  has a value of 2 before proc1 is called. After the call it will have a
  value of 15. If the VAR keyword were left out, int2 would have the
  same value of 2 after the call to proc1. Constants and expressions
  cannot be passed as VAR parameters. The rest of the procedure body
  looks like any other PPE except for the ENDPROC keyword. This is
  specific to procedures and marks the end of the procedure definition.

         Now lets look at an example of a function.

  EXAMPLE 2
  --------

 1  DECLARE FUNCTION func1(STRING str,INTEGER i) STRING
 2
 3  *$USEFUNCS
 4
 5  BEGIN
 6
 7  STRING  s1
 8  INTEGER k
 9
 10       LET s1 = "Is it lunch time yet?"
 11       LET k  = 0
 12
 13       println s1
 14       println func1(s1,k)
 15 END
 16
 17 FUNCTION func1(STRING str, INTEGER i) STRING
 18
 19        if (i = 0) func1 = "No"
 20        if (i = 1) func1 = "Yes"
 21
 22 ENDFUNC

         Functions are declared nearly the same way as procedures with
  one exception. The return type of the function must be specified after
  the parameter list. In this example the function func1 will return a
  STRING as seen on line 1. Function defintions are nearly the same as
  procedures. First, the return type must be specified after the
  parameter list as seen on line 17. Second the keyword ENDFUNC is used
  instead of ENDPROC.

         The big difference between functions and procedures is that
  functions return a value. To assign the return value inside a
  function, simply use the name of the function just like a variable.
  You do not need to declare this variable, it is done for you. When the
  function is finished executing the value in the return variable will
  be made available as the return value.

         Note that function calls can take place anywhere inside of an
  expression as well as stand-alone statements. This can be useful in
  situations where the functions return value is not needed, but the
  function's side effects are.

        Now lets look at some practicle examples. It is a good idea to
  keep related functions and procedures in a seperate file called a
  library. The use of the *$INCLUDE compiler directive allows the
  inclusion of code libraries. Here is an example of a library
  containing some useful math routines.

FILE: MAIN.PPS
-----------------------
  *$USEFUNCS
  *$INCLUDE:MATH.LIB

  BEGIN
       PRINTLN "4 to the 3rd power = ",Xto_theY(4,3)
       PRINTLN "4 squared = ",square(4)
  END

FILE: MATH.LIB
-----------------------
  DECLARE FUNCTION Xto_theY(INTEGER x, INTEGER y) INTEGER
  DECLARE FUNCTION square(INTEGER x) INTEGER

  FUNCTION Xto_theY(INTEGER x, INTEGER y) INTEGER

    INTEGER i
      Xto_theY = x
      for i = 2 to y
        Xto_theY = Xto_theY * x
      next i

  ENDFUNC

  FUNCTION square(INTEGER x) INTEGER

    square = x * x

  ENDFUNC


        It is important that the DECLARE statements for each function or
  procedure appear before a call to that function/procedure. The
  folowing code is ILLEGAL in PPL.

  BEGIN
       PRINTLN "4 squared = ",square(4)
  END

  DECLARE FUNCTION square(INTEGER x) INTEGER

  FUNCTION square(INTEGER x) INTEGER
    square = x * x
  ENDFUNC
  
        Remember to use the *$USEFUNCS compiler directive when using
  functions and procedures. This will allow you to put your main body of
  code anywhere in your source file(s).

  *Beginners note. The main body of code is the code that appears between
  BEGIN
    and
  END

        Now would be a good time to take a close look at PROCEDURES.
  Especially the VAR parameter modifier. Procedures should be used when
  a return value is irrelevant, or if there needs to be several return
  values. The VAR modifier makes this possible. Parameters are passed
  into procedures by name. What this means is that what you pass into a
  procedure is copied into a local variable of the same type. For
  example the procedure call

          COPY_STRING("Hello",str)

  where the procedure COPY_STRING is declared as

          DECLARE PROCEDURE COPY_STRING(STRING s1, VAR STRING s2)

          PROCEDURE COPY_STRING(STRING s1, VAR STRING s2)
             s2 = s1
          ENDPROC

        This is an oversimplified example which illustrates how the VAR
  modifier works. The string "HELLO" is copied into a local variable
  called s1. The string str is copied into a local variable s2.

  *BEGGINERS NOTE
   A local variable is a variable which can only be used within the function
   or procedure in which it is declared. In this case they are formal parameters
   and are declared for you by the compiler.

        The VAR modifier tells PPL to copy the contents of the local
  variable s2 back into str when the procedure is finished processing.
  If the VAR modifier were left out, the contents of s2 would not be
  copied back into str and would be lost. This can be useful when you
  have a parameter variable that must not be modified by a procedure,
  but the procedure needs to temporarily modify the local copy of it for
  some reason.

        Functions cannot use the VAR modifier for two reasons. Number 1
  is because the basic definition of a function is a process which takes
  several values as parameters and returns a single value. Reason number
  2 is a basic limitation in PPL. Because functions return a value,
  memory is set aside to do so. In order to keep PPL backward compatible
  there are only so many bytes to squeeze this information into. There
  just wasn't room to maintain VAR information for functions.

        Another important consideration when using the VAR modifier is
  that of passing constants and expressions as VAR parameters. Think
  about the following example.

  DECLARE PROCEDURE DECREMENT(VAR INTEGER x)
  INTEGER i
  BEGIN
    LET i = 4
    DECREMENT(4)                    ;Illegal!
    DECREMENT(CURCONF())            ;Illegal!
    DECREMENT(i)                    ;Legal
  END

  PROCEDURE DECREMENT(VAR INTEGER x)
    x = x-1
  ENDPROC

        Can you see why this would be a problem? The first call to
  DECREMENT is passing a constant or a constant expression. If this were
  allowed, the procedure DECREMENT would decrement the value of the
  constant 4 by 1. Once this has been done, every time you use the
  constant 4 it would acutally be 3. eg PRINTLN 4 would actually print
  the number 3.

        The second call to DECREMENT(CURCONF()) would try to decrement
  the value returned by CURCONF(). However, there is no variable
  associated with that value. If this were allowed to happen, the value
  returned by CURCONF() would be lost forever in the bit bucket.
  Fortunatly the compiler will catch both of these types of errors. Note
  that if there were no VAR modifier both these examples would be
  perfectly valid.

        The addition of functions and procedures is an exciting addition
  to PPL. Using structured programming can make your PPE's look much
  more organized. Putting often used functions and procedures into
  libraries can help you see your code in an organized way.

  *BEGINNERS NOTE
       A very simple explanation of structured programming
       ---------------------------------------------------

    PPL allows the use of GOTO, GOSUB and multiple logical statements
    per line. The use of these statements leads to unstructured, hard to
    follow code. The following is an example of an unstructured program.

    PRINT "Hello" : goto goodbye
    gosub farewell : call "next.ppe" : PRINT "Boy am I a mess"
    :goodbye
    PRINT "Goodbye" : PRINT "Cruel " : PRINT "World " : end
    :farewell
    PRINTLN "I wish I could say farewell, but I will never be executed"
    RETURN

    You can see how this can turn into a big mess. In fact this piece of
    code will not even execute as you might think at first glance. Here
    is a better example using structured programming.

    DECLARE PROCEDURE  hello()
    DECLARE PROCERDURE goodbye()
    DECLARE PROCEDURE  farewell()

    BEGIN
      hello()
      goodbye()
      farewell()
    END
    PROCEDURE hello()
      PRINTLN "Hello "
    ENDPROC

    PROCEDURE goodbye()
      PRINTLN "Goodbye Cruel World!"
    ENDPROC

    PROCEDURE farewell()
      PRINTLN "Farewell "
    ENDPROC

           You can see how much easier a structured program is to write,
   read and debug. As a general rule of thumb, *never* use GOSUB
   whatever you can do with a GOSUB you can do it better with a function
   or procedure , use GOTO sparingly and stay away from multiple
   statements on single lines unless it really makes sense. eg i = 1 : j
   = 2 : k = 3 makes sense.

    If (i == 3)  then : PRINTLN "i == 3"
    elseif (i == 4)
    PRINTLN "I = 4" : endif

    This dosen't make sense. (I've seen people write code like this too!)


  ===========================================================================

  Section II.            SELECT CASE


        The SELECT CASE construct allows you to organize multiple
  execution paths into a clean, easy to read format. If you are
  unfamiliar with how a BASIC SELECT CASE or C SWITCH construct works
  you can think of it as being similar to a IF-ELSEIF-ELSE construct.

         Here is how a SELECT CASE construct looks in PPL

  1      INTEGER i
  2
  3         LET i = 3
  4
  5          SELECT CASE (i)
  6
  7                CASE 1
  8
  9                       PRINTLN "i = 1"
 10                       proc1(i)
 11
 12                CASE 2,6,10
 13
 14                       PRINTLN "i is 2,6 or 10"
 15                       proc2(i)
 16
 17                CASE 3
 18
 19                       PRINTLN "i is 3"
 20
 21                CASE 11..35
 22
 23                       PRINTLN "i is between 11 and 35"
 24
 25                CASE 50..60,64,78
 26
 27                       PRINTLN "I is between 50 and 60 or 64 or 78
 28
 29                DEFAULT
 30
 31                        PRINTLN "i is not a valid value"
 32
 33           END SELECT



         This syntax is straight foward. Line 5 contains the SELECT CASE
  keyword which is followed by an expression (i). This expression can be
  any valid PPL expression. The parenthesis are optional.

         Each CASE contains one or more expressions delimited by commas.
  Each CASE expression is compared to the SELECT CASE expression
  logically. If it is TRUE the body of the CASE is executed. The CASE
  body can contain as many statements as needed, including function
  calls.Note that ranges include the boundry values. eg 11..35 includes
  11 and 35.

         The DEFAULT case will be executed when none of the other CASE
  expressions evaluate to TRUE. For BASIC programmers, the CASE ELSE is
  also valid instead of DEFAULT.

         Here is a more practical example. Suppose you have a menu from
  which the user selects an option. After reading the users input, a
  SELECT CASE construct can be used to branch to the correct section of
  your PPL code. This is an exellent place to put function and procedure
  calls.

            STRING str
            STRING res

            DISPLAY_MENU()
            INPUT "@X04Enter your selection",str

            SELECT CASE (str)

              CASE "A","a"
                   PRINTLN "Your selection was A"
                   SELECTION_A()                 ; Call procedure A

              CASE "B","b"
                   PRINTLN "Your selection was B"
                   SELECTION_B()                 ; Call procedure B

              CASE "Q","q"
                   PRINTLN "About to end program execution"
                   inputyn("Are you sure you want to quit?",res,@X04
                   if (res == "Y") END           ; Quit program

              CASE ELSE                          ; Case invalid input
              
                   PRINTLN "The choice you entered is not a menu item"
                   PRINTLN "Please enter another selection from the menu"
                   LOG("Invalid menu selection",FALSE)

          You can see how easy this is to write, read and debug compared
  to a coresponding IF-ELSEIF-ELSE construct. The abiltiy to put any
  expression in the SELECT CASE expression, eg SELECT CASE (i), SELECT
  CASE (i-2), SELECT CASE ( (i-j)/y ) , SELECT CASE (U_NAME()) makes
  this a very powerful method of controlling program execution. Also the
  ability to use any expression, range or enumerated list in a CASE
  expression makes life much easier.

         CASE 1,3,9,34
              ...
              ...
         CASE 3*i/j

         CASE 10..30





  ===========================================================================

  Section III            DBase III+

         The addition of DBase III+ support to PPL opens an exciting
  door for PPL programmers. No more reading and parsing text files to
  extract records and fields from your own concoction of a database.
  Indexing will make record access much faster and more efficient.

         The choice of using DBase III+ instead of DBase IV was to
  provide for a wider variety of existing databases. We here at Clark
  Development felt a compromise was needed between an existing DBase
  file base and a newer standard in DBase IV.

         Several intrinsic functions and statements have been added to
  PPL to facilitate database management from within a PPE. All DBase
  functions and statements begin with a D. Most DBase statements have
  function equivalents which return an error status or some other useful
  value. A quick Description of these functions and statements follow.
  Please see the file HOWTODBF.TXT for a slightly more detailed
  introduction to using PPL and  DBase.


     Statements:
     ~~~~~~~~~~~

     DCREATE    channel,name,exclusive,fieldInfo ; create DBF file
     DOPEN      channel,name,exclusive           ; open DBF file
     DCLOSE     channel                          ; close DBF file
     DSETALIAS  channel,name                     ; set DBF alias
     DPACK      channel                          ; pack DBF file
     DLOCK      channel                          ; lock DBF file
     DLOCKF     channel                          ; lock DBF file (same as DLOCK)
     DLOCKR     channel,recno                    ; lock a record
     DLOCKG     channel,recnos,count             ; lock a group of records
     DUNLOCK    channel                          ; unlock any current locks
     DNCREATE   channel,name,expression          ; create NDX file
     DNOPEN     channel,name                     ; open NDX file
     DNCLOSE    channel,name                     ; close NDX file
     DNCLOSEALL channel                          ; close all NDX files
     DNEW       channel                          ; start a new record
     DADD       channel                          ; add the new record
     DAPPEND    channel                          ; append a blank record
     DTOP       channel                          ; go to top record
     DGO        channel,recno                    ; go to specific record
     DBOTTOM    channel                          ; go to bottom record
     DSKIP      channel,number                   ; skip +/- a number of records
     DBLANK     channel                          ; blank the record
     DDELETE    channel                          ; delete the record
     DRECALL    channel                          ; recall the record
     DTAG       channel,name                     ; select a tag
     DSEEK      channel,expression               ; string or double
     DFBLANK    channel,name                     ; blank a named field
     DGET       channel,name,var                 ; get a value from a named field
     DPUT       channel,name,expression          ; put a value to a named field
     DFCOPY     channel,name,channel,name        ; copy a field to a field
     DCLOSEALL                                   ; close all DBF files

     Functions:
     ~~~~~~~~~~
     DRECCOUNT (channel)        (INTEGER)        ; return the number of records
     DRECNO    (channel)        (INTEGER)        ; return the current record number
     DBOF      (channel)        (BOOLEAN)        ; return the begin of file status
     DEOF      (channel)        (BOOLEAN)        ; return the end of file status
     DDELETED  (channel)        (BOOLEAN)        ; return the deleted flag
     DCHANGED  (channel)        (BOOLEAN)        ; return the changed flag
     DFIELDS   (channel)        (INTEGER)        ; return count of fields
     DNAME     (channel,number) (STRING)         ; return name of numbered field
     DTYPE     (channel,name)   (STRING)         ; return type of named field
     DLENGTH   (channel,name)   (INTEGER)        ; return length of named field
     DDECIMALS (channel,name)   (INTEGER)        ; return decimals of named field
     DSELECT   (alias)          (INTEGER)        ; returns channel assiciated with alias
     DSEEK     (channel,expression) (INTEGER)    ; returns error status ( 0|1 )
                                                ; or seek success (0 = Error
                                                ; 1 = success, 2 = following record
                                                ; 3 = end of file )
     DGETALIAS (channel)        (STRING)                 ; return the current alias
     DCLOSEALL                  (BOOLEAN) error status   ; close all DBF files
     DOPEN     (channel,name,exclusive)(BOOLEAN) error   ; open DBF file
     DCLOSE    (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; close DBF file
     DSETALIAS (channel,name)   (BOOLEAN) error status   ; set DBF alias
     DPACK     (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; pack DBF file
     DLOCK     (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; lock DBF file
     DLOCKR    (channel,recno)  (BOOLEAN) error status   ; lock a record
     DUNLOCK   (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; unlock any current locks
     DNOPEN    (channel,name)   (BOOLEAN) error status   ; open NDX file
     DNCLOSE   (channel,name)   (BOOLEAN) error status   ; close NDX file
     DNCLOSEALL(channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; close all NDX files
     DNEW      (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; start a new record
     DADD      (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; add the new record
     DAPPEND   (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; append a blank record
     DTOP      (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; go to top record
     DGO       (channel,recno)  (BOOLEAN) error status   ; go to specific record
     DBOTTOM   (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; go to bottom record
     DSKIP     (channel,number) (BOOLEAN) error status   ; skip +/- a number of records
     DBLANK    (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; blank the record
     DDELETE   (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; delete the record
     DRECALL   (channel)        (BOOLEAN) error status   ; recall the record
     DTAG      (channel,name)   (BOOLEAN) error status   ; select a tag
     DFBLANK   (channel,name)   (BOOLEAN) error status   ; blank a named field
     DGET      (channel,name)   (STRING)                 ; get a value from a named field
     DPUT      (channel,name,expression)(BOOLEAN) error  ; put a value to a named field
     DFCOPY    (channel,name,channel,name)(BOOLEAN) error; copy a field to a field
     DERR      (channel)        (BOOLEAN)        ; return error flag for channel
     DERRMSG   (errcode)(STRING)                 ; returns last DBase error text.

      CAUTION: DBase functions that return the error status actually
              return !ERROR. This is to provide a consistent way to
              express an error in an expression. For example:
              if ( DERR (...)) println "Error!"       ;DERR  returns 1 or TRUE on an error.
              if (!DSEEK(...)) println "Seek failed!" ;DSEEK returns 0 or FALSE on an error.

     KEY:
     *NOTE Where file names are used, file extensions are optional.
           Any extension you provide will be ignored. DBF and IDX are the default.

     channel  :  Any value between 0 and 7
     name     :  Char string
     exclusive:  Integer (TRUE || FALSE)
     fieldinfo:  Character string with the following fields

                 1- Field name
                 2- Field Type
                         C = Character
                         N = Numeric
                         F = Floating Point
                         D = Date
                         L = Logical
                         M = Memo
                 3- Field Length
                 4- Decimal (number of digits to the right of the decimal)

                 EXAMPLE: string finfo(3)
                          let finfo(0) = "First,C,20,0"
                          let finfo(1) = "Last,C,20,0"
                          let finfo(2) = "Phone,C,15,0"
                          *NOTE multiple fields require an array of
                          strings. 1 string for each field.

     expression : Character String with search criteria on a field.


                 EXAMPLE: string expr
                         let expr = "First"

     recno,recnos,number,count : integers


         These intrinsic functions and statements are used in the same
  way as any other PPL functions/statements. It is beyond the scope of this
  document to go into how to program a database. Please see the file
  HOWTODBF.TXT to get started on DBase programming.

  ============================================================================

  Section IV       Miscellaneous Intrinsic Functions and Statements




    FUNCTION  DRIVESPACE.
              Usage: DRIVESPACE(drivespec)
         Return Val: Amount of divespace left of drive drivespec.
            Example: integer left
                     left = DRIVESPACE("c:\")
                     println "There are ",tostring(left)," bytes on drive C."

           Comments: drivespec must include at least a drive letter AND
           a colon. Backslash is optional. With directory specs it will
           work also. valid drivespecs are C: C:\ C:\PCB These will all
           return drivespace left on dirve C *NOTE On LANTASTIC this
           will return drivespace of the current physical drive even if
           it is mapped as a directory.

  =============================================================================
    KEYWORD SETLMR
              Usage: SETLMR conf#,msg#
         Return VAl: None
            Example: Integer conf,msg
                     if(newuser == TRUE) then         ; If new user
                       while(conf < HICONFNUM() ) DO  ; set all LMR's to
                          join conf                   ; HI_MSG - 10
                          SETLMR conf,HIMSGNUM()-10
                          INC conf
                       endwhile
                     endif

           Comments: If conf# is greater than the number of actual
                     confrences conf# will default to the highest
                     conference number If msg# is greater than the
                     highest message number in that conference, it will
                     default to the highest message number in that
                     conference. This could be used to set a new users
                     mesg popinters to recent messages so they aren't
                     replying to 3 year old messages.  A useful feature
                     would be to get the high conference number.

           See Also  HICONFNUM(),HIMSGNUM(),NUMACTMSG(),LOWMSGNUM()

  =============================================================================
    KEYWORD SETENV

              Usage: SETENV env_var
         Return Val: N/A
            Example: string s
                     let s = "stan=Stan"
                     SETENV s
                     ...
                     ..
                     .
                     if (GETENV("stan") = "Stan") then
                       Println "Environment variable stan = Stan "
                     endif

           Comments: Used to set DOS environment variable. This can be
                     used for PPE's to communicate with other PPE's. The
                     environment variables set within PPL will NOT be
                     available to DOORs.  Environment variables set
                     within PPL will be cleared the next time PCBoard
                     recycles through DOS. Care should be taken by the
                     PPL programmer to clear environment variables when
                     possible.

                     see also GETENV, SHELL, CALL


  =============================================================================

    STATEMENT FCLOSEALL

              Usage: Closes all file channels
         Return Val: N/A
            Example: fopen 1, "Autoexec.bat"
                     fopen 2, "Config.sys"
                     .
                     .
                     .
                     fcloseall


           Comments: Closes all file channels.
                     Checking if they are acutally open is taken care
                     of elsewhere.

  ==============================================================================
     FUNCTION FNEXT
               Usage:  println "The next available file channel is ",FNEXT()

          Return Val:  Returns an availble file channel. -1 when none are
                      available.

           Comments:  FNEXT was created in order to better support code
                      libraries made possible by functions and
                      procedures. File channel numbers can now be
                      determined at runtime.

                      *CAUTION Until you actually OPEN a file FNEXT will return
                      the same value over and over.
                      chan1 = FNEXT()
                      chan2 = FNEXT()  WRONG! chan1 will equal chan2

                      another gotcha:
                      FOPEN FNEXT(),blah blah

                         There is no way to determine what channel was used
                         to open the file!

                      Here's an example of how it should be used:

                      chan1 = FNEXT()
                      FOPEN   chan1,...

                      chan2 = FNEXT()
                      FOPEN   chan2,...
================================================================================

    FUNCTION HICONFNUM

              Usage: let i = HICONFNUM()
         Return Val: The highest conference number available on the board
            Example: integer i
                     println "The highest conference available is ",HICONFNUM()


           Comments: If a conference is installed it will be counted,
           even if it is not being used.

  ==============================================================================
    FUNCTION OUTBYTES()

              Usage: OUTBYTES()
         Return Val: returns the number of bytes waiting in the modems output
                     buffer
            Example: integer i
                     println "Bytes waiting in the modem output buffer ",OUTBYTES()


           Comments: Not available in local mode.

  =============================================================================


    FUNCTION INBYTES()

              Usage: INBYTES()
         Return Val: Returns number of bytes waiting in the modem input buffer
            Example: integer i
                     println "Bytes in modem input buffer = ",INBYTES()

           Comments: Not available in local mode.

  =============================================================================

    FUNCTION PCBMAC()

              Usage: str = PCBMAC("@macro@")
         Return Val: Returns a BIGSTR containing the expanded text of a PCB
                     MACRO
            Example: integer i,j, res
                     j = PCBMAC("@Timelimit@")
                     i = PCBMAC("@Timeused@")
                     res = j-i
                     println "You have ",res, " Minutes left"

   PCB MACROS not supported:
   @automore@ @beep@ @clreol@ @cls@ @delay@ @more@ @pause@ @poff@ @pon@ @pos@
   @qoff@ @qon@ @wait@ @who@ @x@



  ===============================================================================

     FUNCTION CRC32()

              Usage: UNSIGNEDTYPE = CRC32(CRC_FILE,"C:\AUTOEXEC,BAT")
                                  = CRC32(CRC_STR,"Stan is super cool")
         Return Val: Returns an UNSIGNED value of the CRC of a file or string.
            Example: Println "CRC on the file AUTOEXEC.BAT is",
                     CRC32(CRC_FILE,"C:\AUTOEXEC.BAT")

           Comments: the constants CRC_FILE and CRC_STR are the same as TRUE
                     and FALSE. They were added to make it easier to see if a
                     file or string was being processed.

                     *Caution. CRC32(CRC_STR,"c:\Autoexec.bat") will return
                     the CRC on the string "C:\AUTOEXEC.BAT"

  =============================================================================


    FUNCTION ACTMSGNUM()

              Usage: ACTMSGNUM()
         Return Val: Returns number of active messages in current conference
            Example: integer i
                     println "There are ",ACTMSGNUM()," messages in conference ",CURCONF()

           Comments:

  =============================================================================
    FUNCTION STACKLEFT()

              Usage: STACKLEFT()
         Return Val: Returns the number of bytes left on the *system* stack.
            Example: println "There are ",STACKLEFT()," bytes left on the stack"

                     ;recursive call support
                     function stan(integer i,string str)
                       if(stackleft() > STK_LIMIT) stan(i,"Debra")
                     endfunc

           Comments: This function was added to support nested and recursive
                     function calls. Since function calls take a lot of stack
                     space. As of now only about 26 nested or recursive calls
                     can eat up the stack. This lets the programmer know when
                     he/she is running out of stack space as to avoid a runtime
                     error. Both recursion and nested function calls should
                     check this value if more than just a few calls are to be
                     executed.

  =============================================================================

     FUNCTION STACKERR()

              Usage: STACKERR()
         Return Val: Returns a boolean value which indicates a stack error
                     has occured if TRUE.

            Example: if (STACKERR()) then
                       println "An error has occured "
                       end
                     endif

           Comments: Because of the limited stack space for recursive and nested
                     function calls this function was created. It allows the
                     programmer to determine if a stack error has occured
                     while executing a PPE. This is in addition to the error
                     message when the error occurs. The only way this will be
                     useful is if the PPL programmer has told PPL not to abort
                     on stack errors. PPL will *not* allow system memory to
                     be corrupted when stack space has been exausted. It will
                     disallow any more function calls when there is no system
                     stack space left.
                     *Note nested/recursive procedure calls are limited by heap
                     space, not stack space.


  =============================================================================

     STATEMENT STACKABORT
              Usage: STACKABORT TRUE | FALSE
         Return Val: N/A

            Example: STACKABORT TRUE (Default is TRUE)

           Comments: This allows the programmer to tell the runtime module
                     to try its best to continue executing after a stack error
                     has occurred. If it is passed FALSE, it will abort
                     execution after a stack error. If it is passed TRUE
                     the PPE will continue to run.
                     *CAUTION! If you continue to execute after a stack
                     error, program execution will be unpredictable. PPL
                     will not allow system memory to be corrupted because of
                     a stack error.

  ============================================================================

     CONSTANT STK_LIMIT
              Usage: if (stackleft() > stk_limit)
                        ;recursive call
         Return Val: N/A

           Comments: This constant was added so the PPL programmer could
                     determine how close they are getting to the stack limit
                     when using recursion.

==============================================================================

     CONSTANT(S) CRC_FILE, CRC_STR

              Usage: CRC32(CRC_FILE,"C:\AUTOEXEC.BAT")
                     CRC32(CRC_STR ,"Hello There Bill")
         Return Val: N/A

           Comments: These constants were added to avoid confusion when telling
                     the function CRC32 what it is taking the CRC of. CRC_FILE
                     tells CRC32 to calculate the CRC of the file contained
                     within the string argument. CRC_STR tells CRC32 to
                     calculate the CRC of the string argument itself.
                     CRC_FILE has a value of 1 (TRUE) CRC_STR has a value of
                     0 (FALSE)


 ==============================================================================

      FUNCTION DNEXT
              Usage:  println "The next available dbase file channel is ",DNEXT()

         Return Val:  Returns an available dbase file channel. -1 when none are
                      available.

           Comments:  DNEXT was created in order to better support code
                      libraries made possible by functions and procedures.
                      File channel numbers can now be determined at runtime.

                      *CAUTION Until you actually OPEN a file DNEXT will return
                      the same value over and over.
                      chan1 = DNEXT()
                      chan2 = DNEXT()  WRONG! chan1 will equal chan2

                      another gotcha:
                      FOPEN DNEXT(),...

                         There is no way to determine what channel was used
                         to open the file!

                      Here's an example of how it should be used:

                      chan1 = DNEXT()
                      FOPEN   chan1,...

                      chan2 = DNEXT()
                      FOPEN   chan2,...
 ===============================================================================

       TYPE DDATE

              Usage: DDATE d1,d2,d3

              Value: Signed long integer for julian date.

           Comments: DDATE is for use with DBase date fields. It holds a long
                     integer for julian dates. When coerced to string type it
                     is in the format CCYYMMDD or 19940527

 ==============================================================================

      FUNCTION TODDATE()
              Usage: DATE d1
                     DDATE d2
                     d2 = TODDATE(d1)

       Return Value: A DDATE type variable.

           Comments: Converts any PPL type to DDATE type.

                     *NOTE this is an explicit type conversion. Implicit
                     type conversion is also valid as with all other PPL types.

==============================================================================


    STATEMENT FREALTUSER
             Usage: FREALTUSER

       Return Value: N/A

           Commnets: Since only one GETALTUSER can be active at one time,
                     FREALTUSER can allow other processes which need to use
                     GETALTUSER (such as the MESSAGE command) to do so.

            Example:string name
                    GETALTUSER 20
                    name = U_NAME()
                    FREALTUSER
                    message 1,name,...


==============================================================================
ACCOUNTING

          Several functions and statements have been added to support PCBoard
accounting features. Also, many system constants have been added to make
using these funtions and statements easier for the PPL programmer.


CONSTANTS
         There are three new functions which return accounting information.
         Each function will return a value based on a parameter passed to it.
         Several constants have been added to make accessing these values
         easier. The following list details these consts and what they are
         used for.

for use with PCBACCOUNT() only!
Val Constant            Assosiated value                       Example
=============================================================================
0  NEWBALANCE      Credits given to a new user account        PCBACCOUNT(NEWBALANCE)
1  CHRG_CALL       Credits charged for a call                 PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_CALL)
2  CHRG_TIME       Credits charged for time used (in minutes) PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_TIME)
3  CHRG_PEAKTIME   Credits charged for peak time used         PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_PEAKTIME)
4  CHRG_CHAT       Credits charged for chat session           PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_CHAT)
5  CHRG_MSGREAD    Credits charged for reading a message      PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_MSGREAD)
6  CHRG_MSGCAP     Credits charged for capturing a message    PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_MSGCAP)
7  CHRG_MSGWRITE   Credits charged for writing a message      PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_MSGWRITE)
8  CHRG_MSGECHOED  Credits charged for writing an echoed message PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_MSGECHOED)
9  CHRG_MSGPRIVATE Credits charged for writing a private message PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_MSGPRIVATE)
10 CHRG_DOWNFILE   Credits charged for downloading a file     PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_DOWNFILE)
11 CHRG_DOWNBYTES  Credits charged for downloading bytes      PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_DOWNBYTES)
12 PAY_UPFILE      Credits given   for uploading a file       PCBACCOUNT(PAY_UPFILE)
13 PAY_UPBYTES     Credits given   for uploading bytes        PCBACCOUNT(PAY_UPBYTES)
14 WARN_LEVEL      Credit  threshold for low credit warning   PCBACCOUNT(WARN_LEVEL)


The following are for use with PCBACCSTAT() only!
val    constant
--------------------------
0      ACC_STAT    Returns status of the "Enable Accounting"  PABACCSTAT(ACC_STAT)
                   switch in the PWRD file.  0=Accounting
                   disabled (N), 1=Tracking (T), and
                   2=Enabled (Y).
1      ACC_TIME    The amount of ADDITIONAL units to charge   PCBACCSTAT(ACC_TIME)
                   per minute while in the current
                   conference.
2      ACC_MSGR    The amount to charge in ADDITION for each  PCBACCSTAT(ACC_MSGREAD)
                   message read in the current conference.
3      ACC_MSGW    The amount to charge in ADDITION for each  PCBACCSTAT(ACC_MSGWRITE)
                   message entered in the current conference.

4      ACC_CUR_BAL Return the up to the minute user balance.

The following are for use with ACCOUNT(), ACCOUNT and RECORDUSAGE only!
val constant       description                                example
----------------------------------------------------------------------
0  START_BAL       Users starting balance.                    ACCOUNT(START_BAL)
1  START_SESSION   Users starting balance for this session    ACCOUNT(START_SESSION)
2  DEB_CALL        Debit for this call                        ACCOUNT DEB_CALL,10
3  DEB_TIME        Debit for time on                          ACCOUNT DEB_TIME,10
4  DEB_MSGREAD     Debit for reading message                  ACCOUNT DEB_MSGREAD,100
5  DEB_MSGCAP      Debit for capturing a message              ACCOUNT(DEB_MSGCAP)
6  DEB_MSGWRITE    Debit for writing a message                ACCOUNT(DEB_MSGWRITE)
7  DEB_MSGECHOED   Debit for echoed message                   ACCOUNT DEB_MSGECHOED 3
8  DEB_MSGPRIVATE  Debit for writing private message          ACCOUNT DEB_MSGPRIVATE 10
9  DEB_DOWNFILE    Debit for downloading a file               ACCOUNT DEB_DOWNFILE 100
10 DEB_DOWNBYTES   Debit for downloading bytes                ACCOUNT(DEB_DOWNBYTES)
11 DEB_CHAT        Debit for chat                             ACCOUNT(DEB_CHAT)
12 DEB_TPU         Debit for TPU                              ACCOUNT(DEB_TPU)
13 DEB_SPECIAL     Debit special                              ACCOUNT(DEB_SPECIAL)
14 CRED_UPFILE     Credit for uploading a file                ACCOUNT(CRED_UPFILE)
15 CRED_UPBYTES    Credit for uploading bytes                 ACCOUNT(CRED_UPBYTES)
16 CRED_SPECIAL    Credit special                             ACCOUNT(CRED_SPECIAL)
17 SEC_DROP        Security level to drop to at 0 credits     ACCOUNT(SEC_DROP)

                This group of constants can be used to access or modify
user account information using the ACCOUNT() function ,ACCOUNT statement and/or
RECORDUSAGE. The ACCOUNT() function returns the current value and the
ACCOUNT statement is used to modify a value. Record usage also modifies a
value with more information stored in a usage file. See description below for
more details. These constants can also be used for the RECORDUSAGE statement
with the acception of the following.
STARTBAL, STARTSESSION, SEC_DROP;


=================================================================================
FUNCTION ACCOUNT(INTEGER field)

         Usage:  println "You have been charged ",ACCOUNT(DEB_CHAT)," for chat"
  Return Value:  Returns amount of credits charged for services corresponding
                 to the field parameter.
    Parameters:  The field number to access (1-14) or using DEB_ constants

      Commnets:  The account function is used to retrieve account information
                 from PCBoard.
                 These are the constants which can be used with the ACCOUNT()
                 function.

Val Constant      Descripttion                                Example
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0  START_BAL       Users starting balance.                    ACCOUNT(START_BAL)
1  START_SESSION   Users starting balance for this session    ACCOUNT(START_SESSION)
2  DEB_CALL        Debit for this call                        ACCOUNT DEB_CALL,10
3  DEB_TIME        Debit for time on                          ACCOUNT DEB_TIME,10
4  DEB_MSGREAD     Debit for reading message                  ACCOUNT DEB_MSGREAD,100
5  DEB_MSGCAP      Debit for capturing a message              ACCOUNT(DEB_MSGCAP)
6  DEB_MSGWRITE    Debit for writing a message                ACCOUNT(DEB_MSGWRITE)
7  DEB_MSGECHOED   Debit for echoed message                   ACCOUNT DEB_MSGECHOED 3
8  DEB_MSGPRIVATE  Debit for writing private message          ACCOUNT DEB_MSGPRIVATE 10
9  DEB_DOWNFILE    Debit for downloading a file               ACCOUNT DEB_DOWNFILE 100
10 DEB_DOWNBYTES   Debit for downloading bytes                ACCOUNT(DEB_DOWNBYTES)
11 DEB_CHAT        Debit for chat                             ACCOUNT(DEB_CHAT)
12 DEB_TPU         Debit for TPU                              ACCOUNT(DEB_TPU)
13 DEB_SPECIAL     Debit special                              ACCOUNT(DEB_SPECIAL)
14 CRED_UPFILE     Credit for uploading a file                ACCOUNT(CRED_UPFILE)
15 CRED_UPBYTES    Credit for uploading bytes                 ACCOUNT(CRED_UPBYTES)
16 CRED_SPECIAL    Credit special                             ACCOUNT(CRED_SPECIAL)
17 SEC_DROP        Security level to drop to at 0 credits     ACCOUNT(SEC_DROP)
=================================================================================
STATEMENT ACCOUNT INTEGER field, INTEGER value


          Usage: ACCOUNT DEB_CHAT,10
     Parameters:  field: A value between 0-14. Using system constants is reccomended.
                  value: The amount of credits to add or subtract to field the field

        Example: Record a use of 10 credits for chatting
                ACCOUNT DEB_CHAT,10

       Commnets: The ACCOUNT statement is used to modify accounting information
                 for a user. This statement will simply modify a debit value
                 whereas the RECORDUSAGE will do the same thing as well as
                 record information in the accounting file.

                 The valid constants for this statement are the same as those
                 used for the ACCOUNT() Function.
=================================================================================
STATEMENT RECORDUSAGE INTEGER field,STRING desc1,STRING desc2,DWORD unitcost,INTEGER value

          Usage: RECORDUSAGE DEB_CHAT,"Debit for chat", "Using PPE",10,10
     Parameters: field  Same as ACCOUNT
                 descr1 Descripttion of charge
                 descr2 Subdescription of charge
                 unitcost cost per unit
                 value  number of units
       Comments: Recordusage will update debit values in PCBoard as well
                 as record descriptions and other information in an accouinting
                 file.
                 Valid values for the field parameter are 2-16. The constants
                 corresponding with these values (DEB_???) could and should
                 be used here.

                 The constants used for this statement are the same as those
                 used for the ACCOUNT() function above.
================================================================================

FUNCTION PCBACCOUNT(INTEGER field)
         Usage: println "You will be charged ",PCBACCOUNT(CHRG_CHAT)," for chat"

    Parameters: field  value from 0-10 designating which field to access

      Commnets:
                This function returns what PCBoard will charge a user for a
                certian activity. These are values the SysOp assigns in
                PCBsetup when accounting is configures and enabled.
                Valid values for the field paramter are 0-14. Use of the
                corresponding constants is encouraged.

                Here are the fileds PCBACCOUNT can access in the accounting
                configuration.

Val    PPL Constant       FIELD                        Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0      NEWBALANCE        New user balance              Credits given to a new user account
1      CHRG_CALL         Charge for call               Credits charged for a call
2      CHRG_TIME         Charge for time               Credits charged for time used (in mi
3      CHRG_PEAKTIME     Peak time charge              Credits charged for peak time used
4      CHRG_CHAT         Charge for chat               Credits charged for chat session
5      CHRG_MSGREAD      Message read  CHARGE          Credits charged for reading a messag
6      CHRG_MSGCAP       Charge for message capture    Credits charged for capturing a mess
7      CHRG_MSGWRITE     Charge for writing a message  Credits charged for writing a messag
8      CHRG_MSGECHOED    Charge for echoing a message  Credits charged for writing an echoe
9      CHRG_MSGPRIVATE   Charge for leaving privatemsg Credits charged for writing a privat
10     CHRG_DOWNFILE     Charge for downloading a file Credits charged for downloading a fi
11     CHRG_DOWNBYTES    Charge dor downloading bytes  Credits charged for downloading byte
12     PAY_UPFILE        Credit for uploading a file   Credits given   for uploading a file
13     PAY_UPBYTES       Credit for uploading bytes    Credits given   for uploading bytes

       These fields are not modifiable from within PPL. Only in PCBsetup.

===========================================================================

  FUNCTION  PCBACCSTAT(INTEGER field)

        Usage: PRINTLN "Mutiplier for credits is ",PCBACCSTAT(ACC_STAT)
 Return Value: Value in status field

     Comments: This function can and should be used in conjunction with the
               ACC_??? constants as the field parameter.
               Valid values for field are 0-3.
val    constant
--------------------------
0      ACC_STAT    Returns status of the "Enable Accounting"  PABACCSTAT(ACC_STAT)
                   switch in the PWRD file.  0=Accounting
                   disabled (N), 1=Tracking (T), and
                   2=Enabled (Y).
1      ACC_TIME    The amount of ADDITIONAL units to charge   PCBACCSTAT(ACC_TIME)
                   per minute while in the current
                   conference.
2      ACC_MSGR    The amount to charge in ADDITION for each  PCBACCSTAT(ACC_MSGREAD)
                   message read in the current conference.
3      ACC_MSGW    The amount to charge in ADDITION for each  PCBACCSTAT(ACC_MSGWRITE)
                   message entered in the current conference.
4      ACC_CUR_BAL Current up to the minute user balance


===========================================================================
Message Base Access

===========================================================================
 CONSTANTS Message header field access constants.

    Field      Value hex    dec    Field Description
===========================================================================

    HDR_ACTIVE       0x0E   14     Message active flag field
    HDR_BLOCKS       0x04    4     Number of 128 byte blocks in message
    HDR_DATE         0x05    5     Date message was written
    HDR_ECHO         0x0F   15     Echoed message flag
    HDR_FROM         0x0B   11     Who the message is from
    HDR_MSGNUM       0x02    2     Message number
    HDR_MSGREF       0x03    3     Reference message
    HDR_PWD          0x0D   13     Message password
    HDR_REPLY        0x0A   10     Message reply flag
    HDR_RPLYDATE     0x08    8     Reply message date
    HDR_RPLYTIME     0x09    9     Reply message time
    HDR_STATUS       0x01    1     Message status
    HDR_SUBJ         0x0C   12     Message subject
    HDR_TIME         0x06    6     Message time
    HDR_TO           0x07    7     Who the message is to.

    These constants are for use with SCANMSGHDR(conf_num,start_msg,field,text)
in the FIELD parameter.

===============================================================================

FUNCTION SCANMSGHDR(conf,start_msg,field,test)

        Usage: integer msgno
               msgno = SCANMSGHDR(0,1,HDR_TO,"Stan")
 Return Value: The first message number in the message base which matches
               the search criteria.

      Example: ;Searching for the first message with a subject of PPL
               ;in conference 0 starting at message 100
               INTEGER num
               num = SCANMSGHDR(0,100,HDR_SUBJ,"PPL")

     Comments: This function can be used to scan PCBoard message basses
               for certian information. All fields in the standard header
               can be searched. There are 15 fields in the standard
               header. Valid values for field are 1-15. See the section
               on PPL constants related to this function.

               See also MSGTOFILE,Constants HDR_????

===========================================================================

 STATEMENT MSGTOFILE conf,msg_no,filename

        Usage: MSGTOFILE 0,20,"msg.txt"

      Example: ;Using SCANMSGHDR to search for a message
               MSGTOFILE 0,200,"d:\msg1.txt"
               DISPFILE "D:\msg1.txt",DEFS

     comments: This statement will take the given message and write it to a
               text file. The file's first 15 lines will contain standard
               header information. (One field per line) The headers are formatted
               to make parsing easier. The 16th line will state how many
               extended headers are present. The following line(s) will contain
               extended headers. (one per line) Finally, after the extended
               headers, will be a line containing "Message body:". Everything
               after that is the body of the message.

           See also SCANMSGHDR,DISPFILE,Constants HDR_????
===========================================================================

* Important note. You *must* use GETUSER AND PUTUSER with these QWK functions.

STATEMENT QWKLIMITS field,limit

        Usage: QWKLIMITS field,limit

      Example:
              GETUSER
              QWKLIMITS MAXMSGS,500
              PUTUSER

     comments: This statement allows the PPL programmer to modify a users
               QWK limits. Four fields can be modified with thei statement.

               - Max Messages: Maximum messages allowed in a qwk packet
                 * Note: If you specify a number higher than that contained
                         in PCBSETUP the values in PCBSETUP will be used.

               - Max Messages per Conference: Maximum messages allowed in
                 a qwk packet per conference.
                 * Note: If you specify a number higher than that contained
                         in PCBSETUP the values in PCBSETUP will be used.

               - Personal Attach Limit: Maximum number of bytes in attached files
                 for the user.

               - Public Attach Limit: Maximum number of bytes in attached files
                 for the user.

                Four constants have been defined to identify the FIELD value.

                Constant        Value     Field
                MAXMSGS         0         Max messages per qwk packet
                CMAXMSGS        1         Max Messages per conference
                ATTACH_LIM_U    2         Personal attach bytes limit
                ATTACH_LIM_P    3         Public attach bytes limit
                                      ^___________________________________
FUNCTION QWKLIMITS(field)                                                 |
                                                                          |
         Example                                                          |
                GETUSER                                                   |
                PRINTLN QWKLIMITS(MAXMSGS)                                |
                                                                          |
       Comments                                                           |
               This functions returns the values contained in the users   |
               QWK configuration. The same constants used in the QWKLIMITS|
               statements can be used with the field parameter. -----------
